WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:02.627
This is the typical demand for electricity

00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:04.629
on a spring day in California.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:08.591
It starts growing around 6 AM,
then rises again around 6 PM.

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:11.344
Now, this line shows when 
wind power

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.762
feeds the California power grid.

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:15.390
Wind is variable but often 
picks up at night.

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:17.726
Solar panels, 
on the other hand

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:19.394
kick into gear around 7 AM

00:00:19.394 --> 00:00:22.939
generate a lot of electricity until 
the sun sets around 7 PM.

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:24.024
On most days

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.818
neither comes close to meeting 
the peak demands of the day

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.404
so the power company 
relies on fossil fuels

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:31.531
like natural gas to make up for the gap

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:34.701
which widens significantly
when people use electricity the most.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:39.164
Since the power company can't store 
the solar and wind energy

00:00:39.164 --> 00:00:41.374
it has to use fossil fuels 
at these times

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.835
which can be stored 
in barrels and tanks.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.046
It's kind of the big gap

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.714
in our renewable energy system 
right now.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:50.884
If we don't figure out a way storing energy,
there's a chance that we're going

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:53.970
to be still dependent on fossil fuels.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.516
So how do we store some of the solar 
and wind energy for later?

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:01.311
Right now, you might be thinking

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:03.396
“Just use a battery.” 
and you're not wrong.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.857
Batteries have improved immensely
over the past few years

00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:07.567
particularly lithium-ion batteries

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.819
which use a chemical reaction 
to store energy.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:12.030
Individual homes that have solar panels

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.407
often use lithium-ion batteries 
to store energy.

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:16.534
But there's a few reasons lithium-ion

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:18.161
isn’t perfect for the grid.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:19.662
This is Neel Dhanesha

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:22.082
a founding writer at Heatmap, 
a climate news site.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.459
And he wrote about this 
for Vox in 2022.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.045
One is just the scale that’s needed.

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:29.422
We would need a lot 
at a level

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.049
that we just don't really have 
right now.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:33.551
That's a challenge because lithium 
is only found

00:01:33.551 --> 00:01:35.053
in a few places on Earth.

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:36.012
But more importantly

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:38.598
like we need lithium-ion batteries 
for other things.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.225
Lithium-ion batteries are 
really good for stuff

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:43.061
that moves because 
it's relatively light.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:46.189
Meaning it's better suited for things 
like electric cars

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:47.816
and portable electronics.

00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:49.484
Not power grids that stay still.

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:51.778
Luckily, there's another 
energy storage solution

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.696
that's actually been around 
for a long time.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.575
“This is the site for the first
pump storage 

00:01:57.575 --> 00:01:59.577
hydroelectric station 
in southern Ireland.”

00:01:59.828 --> 00:02:02.997
This is a type of energy storage called 
“Pumped storage hydro”.

00:02:03.248 --> 00:02:04.541
They were first built in Europe.

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:07.585
The US built one in 1929 and 
many more were built

00:02:07.585 --> 00:02:10.505
in the 1970s and 80s as a way 
to stored nuclear power.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.882
Today, these facilities are 
all over the world.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.677
There are 39 of them in the US.

00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:19.013
And they store energy in a really 
fascinating yet simple way.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.475
When energy demand is low, 
renewable or fossil fuel energy

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.894
is used to pump water from 
a reservoir or river

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:27.188
up a mountain into 
a higher reservoir.

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.274
Basically converting this energy

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:31.234
into what's called potential energy.

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:32.193
So potential energy

00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:33.945
you might kind of remember from 
high school physics.

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:37.615
When a thing is up at a height,
it has stored potential energy.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:39.909
When it's let go, it turns into 
kinetic energy.

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:41.369
When that energy is needed

00:02:41.369 --> 00:02:43.246
the water is released down
the mountain

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:45.165
where it's converted into kinetic energy

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:48.042
that spins a turbine and generates
electricity for the grid.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.839
It's a way of combining water, a mountain, 
and gravity into a battery

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.883
and it can be about 90% efficient

00:02:54.883 --> 00:02:57.969
meaning only 10% of this energy 
is lost in the process.

00:02:58.011 --> 00:02:59.888
Pumped torage hydro 
works really well

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:02.265
but it's difficult to build more.

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:04.809
Well, for starters, you need a mountain

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:07.812
and you need to hollow out a mountain
to put pumps inside it.

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:09.814
And it takes a lot of money

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:11.649
and we don't have mountains everywhere.

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:14.861
So the ideal way to store renewable energy 
would be something

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:17.739
that's cheaper and smaller than 
a pumped storage hydro plant...

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:20.450
but works in roughly the same way.

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:22.660
One company, Energy Vault

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:25.205
is also using gravity to store 
renewable energy.

00:03:25.246 --> 00:03:27.207
But without the water or mountain.

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:30.627
Instead, renewable energy is used 
to lift heavy block of concrete

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:31.669
up into the air

00:03:31.669 --> 00:03:33.254
where it becomes potential energy.

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:35.298
Then, when it's needed

00:03:35.298 --> 00:03:37.383
he blocks are released, 
spinning a turbine

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:40.386
which converts the potential energy 
back into electricity.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.682
Energy Vault calls this 
“gravity energy storage”.

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:47.310
And while it's still being tested, 
the company claims

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.479
it could be more than 80% efficient.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.065
A company called Quidnet

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.442
is working on a different version 
of the same principle.

00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:57.820
Their “geomechanical pumped storage” unit 
uses renewable energy

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.406
to pump water underground 
into a pressurized hole...

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:02.992
where it can be stored 
as potential energy.

00:04:03.326 --> 00:04:06.037
Then released back up to the surface 
to spin a turbine

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:07.747
and generate electricity.

00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:11.709
Both techniques are betting on
potential energy as a solution

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:13.419
for storing renewable energy 
for the grid.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:16.089
I think this partly because 
potential energy

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:17.924
has shown itself to be 
pretty efficient.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.343
Also, realistically, fewer moving parts.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.220
If all you're doing basically 
is letting—

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.555
is using gravity 
to work with you...

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:27.976
you have a pretty massive
force of nature

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:29.143
on your side right there.

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:31.896
But potential energy isn't the only 
possible solution.

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:35.525
Other companies are using renewable energy 
to super heat salt...

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.610
insulating it, then 
releasing that heat

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.821
to create steam or hot air 
to drive a turbine.

00:04:40.697 --> 00:04:41.990
Basically storing renewables

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.075
by converting them 
to thermal energy.

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:46.744
Then there's a company that's betting 
on... rusted iron.

00:04:46.995 --> 00:04:48.871
The they're called iron air batteries.

00:04:48.871 --> 00:04:51.833
And what they do is they utilize
the chemical reaction

00:04:51.833 --> 00:04:55.336
that creates rust to store 
and discharge energy.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.755
The thing that we all sort of think of 
as an inconvenience

00:04:57.755 --> 00:05:00.466
could be really useful is just kind of 
beautiful to me.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.219
Right now these ideas are all 
in various stages of development.

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:05.513
But they are attracting investors.

00:05:05.513 --> 00:05:07.724
And the hope is that several will work

00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:10.101
because one might not fit 
every power grid.

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:12.145
The grid, like renewable energy overall

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.647
is going to be a sort of 
patchwork solution.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.774
And so the more we try
these solutions

00:05:16.774 --> 00:05:19.319
the closer we get to figuring out
exactly what the right mix is

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:21.779
for what we need for the 
grid of the future.

